. * FILTERS * (Please adapt according to your needs)
.  
.         *Save only subset containing valid values of pli0040, pli0043_h, pli0
> 044_h, and pli0049_h with exactly one child
.         keep if ((pli0040 > 0 & pli0040 != .) | (pli0043_h > 0 & pli0043_h !=
>  .) | (pli0044_h > 0 & pli0044_h != .) | (pli0049_h > 0 & pli0049_h != .)) & 
> (kidsex02 == -2 & kidsex01 > 0 & !missing(kidsex01))
(564,976 observations deleted)

.         
.         *For each household task time variable...
.         foreach var of varlist pli0040 pli0043_h pli0044_h pli0049_h {
  2.                 
.                 *Convert 'does not apply' to zero (a task you don't do takes 
> no time)
.                 replace `var' = 0 if `var' == -2
  3.                 
.                 *Replace remaining missing values with actual missings
.                 replace `var' = . if `var' < 0
  4.                 
.         }
(890 real changes made)
(1,219 real changes made, 1,219 to missing)
(906 real changes made)
(694 real changes made, 694 to missing)
(3,817 real changes made)
(24 real changes made, 24 to missing)
(2,592 real changes made)
(125 real changes made, 125 to missing)

.         
.         *Generate household task values for each gender
.         gen H_errands_woman = pli0040*(sex == 2)
(1,219 missing values generated)

.         gen H_errands_man = pli0040*(sex == 1)
(1,219 missing values generated)

.         gen H_housework_woman = pli0043_h*(sex == 2)
(694 missing values generated)

.         gen H_housework_man = pli0043_h*(sex == 1)
(694 missing values generated)

.         gen H_childcare_woman = pli0044_h*(sex == 2)
(24 missing values generated)

.         gen H_childcare_man = pli0044_h*(sex == 1)
(24 missing values generated)

.         gen H_repairs_woman = pli0049_h*(sex == 2)
(125 missing values generated)

.         gen H_repairs_man = pli0049_h*(sex == 1)
(125 missing values generated)

.         
.         *Collapse to the original household level
.         collapse (mean) pgmonth kidsex01 (max) kidgeb01 kidmon01 (sum) H_erra
> nds_woman H_errands_man H_housework_woman H_housework_man H_childcare_woman H
> _childcare_man H_repairs_woman H_repairs_man, by(cid syear)

.         
.         *Dropping observations who have not already given birth to their baby
>  at the time of the survey
.         drop if syear < kidgeb01
(6,029 observations deleted)

.         drop if (pgmonth < kidmon01 & syear == kidgeb01 & kidmon01 > 0 & !mis
> sing(kidmon01)) 
(851 observations deleted)

.         *Kids with unknown birth months but born in the same year as the surv
> ey are assumed to be already born and zero years old
.         
.         *Dropping if year of child birth is not reported
.         drop if kidgeb01 < 0
(85 observations deleted)

. 
. * GENERATE VARIABLES * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
.         *Generate male housework share
.         gen male_housework_share = (H_errands_man + H_housework_man + H_child
> care_man + H_repairs_man)/(H_errands_man + H_housework_man + H_childcare_man 
> + H_repairs_man + H_errands_woman + H_housework_woman + H_childcare_woman + H
> _repairs_woman)
(4 missing values generated)

.         
.         *Code first-child-daughter dummy
.         gen first_child_daughter = (kidsex01 == 2)

.         
.         *Code age of child in years
.         gen surveymonth = ym(syear, pgmonth)

.         gen childbirthmonth = ym(kidgeb01, kidmon01)
(24,217 missing values generated)

.         gen age_of_child = floor((surveymonth - childbirthmonth)/12)
(24,217 missing values generated)

.         replace age_of_child = syear - kidgeb01 if missing(age_of_child)
(24,217 real changes made)

.         
.         *Rename survey year and household ID
.         rename syear year

.         rename cid household_ID

.         
. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H1 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
.         *Testing the code we pre-registered, it appears that the margins() co
> mmand does not evaluate the average marginal effect of first_child_daughter o
> n standard_attitude_respondent. We suspect that this is because we only have 
> 5% of the dataset at the moment, and treating child age as a factor variable 
> when interacting with first_child_daughter could be yielding a sparsity issue
> , where not enough children are observed with the same age but different gend
> ers. This could potentially be remedied with the receipt of the full data. We
>  thus implement a conditional strategy: if the margins() average marginal eff
> ect evaluates, then we will use that average marginal effect, as it comes fro
> m the model we pre-registered. If it does not evaluate, then we will change c
> hild age from being treated as a factor variable to being treated as a contin
> uous variable. Our testing implies that this parameterization will yield a de
> fined average marginal effect estimate. We also remove the respondent's age a
> nd gender because they are not defined at the household level, and change the
>  cluster from the personal to household ID
.         
.         *Original pre-registered model
.         reg male_housework_share i.first_child_daughter##i.age_of_child i.yea
> r, vce(cluster household_ID)
